-privacy = f'''<p class="doc-intro">This policy explains the difference between this informational website and the locally operated Cafe Replenishment application, including its optional Gmail connection.</p>
-<p class="updated">Effective date: 22 October 2026 · Application: Cafe Replenishment</p>
-<h2 id="operator">1. The project and this website</h2><p>Cafe Replenishment is an independently maintained café replenishment demonstration project. The public website provides an overview, this privacy policy and terms. It does not provide an application account, receive sales or inventory uploads, collect OAuth credentials, or operate the local replenishment workbench.</p><p>The hosting provider may process ordinary request information such as IP addresses, browser information, access logs or platform session data. The site does not add its own advertising trackers or analytics scripts.</p>
-<h2 id="local">2. Information in the local application</h2><p>The application currently uses synthetic sales and inventory for demonstrations. On the operator’s computer it stores recipes, ingredient stock, supplier contact details, demand forecasts, procurement lists, approval history, supplier draft contents and API export status. The operator controls this local runtime directory.</p>
-<h2 id="gmail">3. Google information and Gmail permissions</h2><p>Connecting Gmail is optional and begins from the local owner workbench. The application requests <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.compose</code>. This Google permission includes managing drafts and sending email. The current application uses it to check connectivity and create procurement drafts; it has no email-sending operation.</p><p>A connection check requests a list containing at most one draft’s identifiers. It does not fetch message bodies. Creating a draft submits the owner-approved supplier recipient, email subject and message body to Google. Gmail returns draft and message identifiers, which are stored locally with export status. The application does not import the inbox or read received email content.</p>
-<h2 id="credentials">4. Authorization credentials and automatic refresh</h2><p>OAuth client credentials, access tokens and refresh tokens are stored by the local server. On Windows they are protected using current-user DPAPI. Other supported platforms use a file with restricted permissions; this is not described as encryption. Credentials are not returned to the phone workbench, embedded in this website, included in supplier messages, or intentionally recorded in application logs.</p><p>When needed for a connection check or draft creation, the local server sends the refresh token to Google’s token service to obtain an access token. Authorization is not permanent: Google test-mode limits, revocation, password changes or account policies may require another browser authorization. The owner can configure service credentials through environment variables; those overrides are managed separately.</p>
-<h2 id="sharing">5. Other services and AI processing</h2><p>If enabled, Runpod receives selected forecasting inputs: product identifiers, demand estimates, permitted adjustment limits and selected weather and holiday context. The current application does not send Google-derived Gmail information, Google OAuth credentials, supplier contacts or procurement email bodies to Runpod, and does not use Google user data to train AI models.</p><p>Weather requests use the configured city or coordinates with the weather provider. A WhatsApp prefilled link includes the supplier phone number and draft text when the owner opens that link. Sending a WhatsApp message remains the owner’s action. Google, Runpod, the weather provider and WhatsApp process submitted information under their own policies.</p>
-<h2 id="limited-use">6. Google API Limited Use</h2><p>Cafe Replenishment’s use and transfer to any other app of information received from Google APIs will adhere to the <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>, including its Limited Use requirements. Google user data is used to provide the requested Gmail draft functionality. It is not sold, used for advertising, or used to train generalized AI models.</p>
-<h2 id="retention">7. Retention, disconnection and deletion</h2><p>Local procurement and approval records remain in the runtime directory until the operator deletes them. Successful draft identifiers and export statuses remain so the application can avoid duplicate creation. There is no scheduled automatic purge.</p><p>“Disconnect Gmail” attempts to revoke the Google authorization and clears locally saved access and refresh tokens. It retains desktop client settings, local procurement records and draft content. If Google revocation cannot be confirmed, the workbench reports that uncertainty. Credentials supplied by environment variables must also be removed from the launch environment.</p><p>You can revoke the application from <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections">Google Account third-party connections</a>. Revocation does not delete drafts already saved in Gmail. Delete those in Gmail, and remove local records from the runtime directory when no longer needed. Close the local server before deleting its runtime files.</p>
-<h2 id="contact">8. Contact and policy changes</h2><p>{CONTACT}</p><p>Policy changes will be reflected on this page with an updated effective date. Material changes to how the application uses Google data require an updated disclosure and any authorization required by Google.</p><div class="notice" lang="zh-CN">中文摘要：网站只介绍项目；库存与采购记录保留在本机。Gmail 授权用于检查连接和创建已审批草稿；Google 权限本身包含发送能力，但当前程序没有发送操作。Google 数据与令牌不用于 AI 训练。你可以在工作台断开授权，并到 Google 账户撤销；已生成草稿需在 Gmail 中自行删除。</div>'''
